13:06:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Good question—it comes down to fundamental differences in how the video is created and compressed. ## Encoding Efficiency **YouTube/streaming video** is encoded with tons of time and computational resources: - Videos are encoded once, then viewed millions of times - YouTube can use slow, computationally expensive encoding (multi-pass encoding) that takes way longer than real-time to process - They can afford to spend massive compute analyzing the entire video to optimize compression frame-by-frame - Modern codecs (VP9, AV1) achieve excellent quality-to-bitrate ratios, but require heavy processing **Game streaming (Stadia, GeForce Now, etc.)** has to encode in real-time: - The image must be captured, encoded, sent, decoded, and displayed within milliseconds to keep latency low - There's no time for multi-pass analysis or complex optimization - Encoding must happen live using hardware encoders (NVENC, etc.) that prioritize speed over compression efficiency - This "single-pass" real-time encoding is inherently less efficient than offline encoding ## Content Differences - **Game footage** often has more motion, fine details, particle effects, and text/UI elements that are harder to compress than typical video content - **Static/predictable scenes** in normal video (like talking heads) compress extremely well; games have constantly changing, less predictable visuals - **Camera movement in games** (especially fast FPS movement) creates motion patterns that don't compress as efficiently as typical cinematic camera work ## Latency Requirements - Streaming services can buffer several seconds of video, allowing the encoder to "look ahead" and make smarter compression decisions - Game streaming needs near-instant transmission, so buffering is minimal to keep input lag low, removing that lookahead advantage ## Bottom Line Essentially, YouTube trades encoding time for efficiency (since it's done once ahead of time), while cloud gaming must trade efficiency for speed (since it's happening live, every single frame, in real-time). This is why Stadia at 1080p could use similar or more bandwidth than a 4K YouTube video.
