13:10:32 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: The user's actually right—4K TVs and 4K monitors do have the same pixel count by convention, and that's exactly what I've been saying about 1080p Stadia and 1080p YouTube. I think there's been a misunderstanding about my point—I'm not claiming the resolutions are different, just that they're equivalent in terms of actual pixel count. The real distinction I should be making is that TV/monitor resolution describes a fixed hardware characteristic of the display panel itself, whereas video resolution describes the transmitted content stream. A 4K TV's resolution is immutable hardware specification, but a video stream's resolution is a content specification that doesn't necessarily tell you anything about the bitrate or quality of what's being encoded within those frame dimensions. These are fundamentally different types of claims—one about display capability, one about digital content. And actually, this bitrate variance isn't unique to Stadia at all. A 4K Blu-ray and a 4K Netflix stream can look dramatically different in quality despite identical resolution, precisely because of compression differences. This happens with all digital video; it's just that people don't usually notice or discuss it because the quality gaps aren't usually as stark as they are with real-time cloud gaming. I should acknowledge that the resolution labels themselves are consistent—2160p is 2160p—but clarify that I'm pointing to a different variable entirely: bitrate, which these labels were never designed to capture.
13:10:32 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Actually, this happens with 4K and 1440p too — you're just not noticing it because the differences are usually less dramatic. **4K Blu-ray vs 4K Netflix stream**: Both are 3840x2160 pixels. But Blu-ray typically uses 60-100+ Mbps, while Netflix 4K streams around 15-25 Mbps. Same resolution, wildly different data rates, and yes, you can usually tell the difference in fine detail/artifacting if you look closely (especially in fast motion or dark scenes). **1440p monitor vs 1440p YouTube video**: A native 1440p monitor is a fixed hardware grid — that's genuinely constant, no variance possible, because it's describing a physical panel, not a content stream. That's actually the key distinction you're bumping into: **monitor/TV resolution is a hardware spec** (the physical arrangement of pixels on a screen, fixed and immutable). **Video resolution is a content spec** (the dimensions of the video file/stream being displayed on that screen). A 4K TV is always physically 4K regardless of what's playing on it — that part genuinely never varies. But what's being *sent to* that screen (bitrate, compression quality) can vary a lot, even while matching the same content resolution. So it's not that Stadia is some weird exception — it's that TV/monitor specs describe hardware (truly fixed), while streaming/game resolution describes content going into that hardware (which can vary in quality at the same resolution). You've been treating both as the same category of claim, but they're answering different questions: - "What's the display capable of?" → hardware resolution, fixed - "What resolution was this specific video encoded at?" → content resolution, fixed number, but says nothing about the data used to fill it
